a thousand miles off the coast of  California halfway to Hawaii and  scientists are trawling for plastic this  is the center of the Great Pacific  Garbage Patch the largest accumulation  of ocean plastic on the planet and the  most comprehensive ever survey show  there's even more debris out there than  feared by draging nets along the surface  in hundreds of locations with further  analysis from the air scientists working  with the ocean cleanup foundation have  calculated there are 1.8 trillion pieces  of plastic weighing 80 thousand tons but  up to 16 times more than previous  estimates so many different you know  size and shapes and you can you know you  can find objects that you may see in  your kitchen right and so you so far  from landings and so it's so alarming  and then you know you have like those  larger debris and all those nets and you  found dead fish around it and it's you  know we're really causing harm out there  the Pacific Garbage Patch is the largest  of five hotspots around the world where  ocean currents sweep together plastic  the new research produced density maps  with red the highest concentration  showing it's now three times the size of  France drifting fishing nets that have  been lost overboard or abandoned make up  much of the plastic the scientists also  found toys household bottles and  containers some with production dates  stretching back to the 1970s British  scientists are horrified once plastic  enters the ocean it can persist for we  think thousands of years it doesn't ever  go away it just breaks down into smaller  and smaller particles to it into micro  plastics and as we've seen these micro  plastics are able to enter the food  chain later this year the ocean cleanup  foundation will launch a long floating  collection screen which will be left in  the Garbage Patch Engineers believe a  fleet of the devices could remove 50  percent of the plastic in five years  other scientists are skeptical and argue  it's better to keep plastic out of the  ocean in the first place  Thomas More Sky News
 

